  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 123 PM EST Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: We'll be in a bit of a rinse-repeat pattern through at \nleast Tuesday. Two upper lows will merge early this week, and \npatches of embedded moisture will result in showery conditions. \nThere will be lulls in activity, though, with the greatest chances \nof limited shower activity Sunday afternoon and Tuesday morning. \nDuring the day on Tuesday, a shortwave ridge will build ahead of an \nincoming low that will open into a trough as it shifts east-\nsoutheast. This will result in flattened 500mb heights with a trend \ntowards decreasing heading into the middle of next week. Forecast \nhigh temperatures will be in the mid-40s to mid-50s, and overnight \nlows will range near 30 to near 40 for the next 3-4 days.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Cyclonic flow centered over eastern Canada will swing a \ncouple shortwaves through northern New York and Vermont next mid \nweek. The first is modeled to swing through on Tuesday night, \nresulting in chance of precipitation 30-60%, locally 55-85% across \nthe northern Greens. As temperatures fall into the upper 20s to mid \n30s, some of the higher elevations could see some snow mixing in \nwith the rain early Wednesday morning. Depending on the strength of \nhigh pressure to our south, there's the chance for additional \nshowers during the daytime Wednesday hours as well (15-45% chance), \nthough most spots should see temperatures warm into the 40s and \nlower 50s that afternoon, resulting in mainly rain. Model \nconsensus really falls apart by Wednesday night, but the main \nagreements relate to brief weak ridging over the area into \nThursday, then a deep trough or closed low developing over the \nmidwest towards Friday. Based on this, chances for precip run \n10-40% for the late week accompanied by highs upper 30s to near \n50 F and lows in the teens to near 30 F. Models are suggesting a\ncoastal storm could set up next weekend to bring more steady \nprecipitation to the region, but the details are not clear that \nfar out.",
